            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Rescales values so that their mean is 0 and their spread (standard deviation) is 1:\neach value becomes how many standard deviations it is above or below the average.\n\nFeatures measured in different units - a height in metres, a speed in km/h, an energy in joules - cannot be compared or combined directly; their z-scores can. Use it before combining features, clustering (scipy.kmeans) or comparing distances, and to spot outliers - a z-score beyond about 3 is rare.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.zscore <axis>\nDefault: axis None (every value together).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.zscore 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values (a list or array). Every input is rescaled and sent. nan values are skipped in working out the mean and spread, and stay nan.\n\naxis (text box, also an inlet):\nNone rescales everything together. A number rescales along that axis: 0 rescales each column separately - each feature on its own scale.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nz:\nThe rescaled values, the same shape. A column that never changes has no spread; its values come out as 0.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour people's heights (metres) and weights (kg) as two columns, rescaled down each column (axis 0). The two columns, a hundred times different in size, come out on the same scale: about -1.3 to 1.4 each.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.mean and np.std give the mean and spread. ranger maps one range onto another. np.percentile gives a scale that ignores outliers.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Rescales values so that their mean is 0 and their spread (standard deviation) is 1:\neach value becomes how many standard deviations it is above or below the average.\n\nFeatures measured in different units - a height in metres, a speed in km/h, an energy in joules - cannot be compared or combined directly; their z-scores can. Use it before combining features, clustering (scipy.kmeans) or comparing distances, and to spot outliers - a z-score beyond about 3 is rare.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.zscore <axis>\nDefault: axis None (every value together).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.zscore 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values (a list or array). Every input is rescaled and sent. nan values are skipped in working out the mean and spread, and stay nan.\n\naxis (text box, also an inlet):\nNone rescales everything together. A number rescales along that axis: 0 rescales each column separately - each feature on its own scale.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nz:\nThe rescaled values, the same shape. A column that never changes has no spread; its values come out as 0.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour people's heights (metres) and weights (kg) as two columns, rescaled down each column (axis 0). The two columns, a hundred times different in size, come out on the same scale: about -1.3 to 1.4 each.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.mean and np.std give the mean and spread. ranger maps one range onto another. np.percentile gives a scale that ignores outliers."
        },
